WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.100
Rocking an 85-pound exoskeleton&nbsp;
and getting some tough love from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.008
Tom Cruise. This is how Emily Blunt got&nbsp;
into tip-top shape for "Edge of Tomorrow!"

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:15.780
In order to prepare for the role of Rita, Emily&nbsp;
Blunt trained for two and a half months prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.220
to shooting. She worked with Jason Walsh, a&nbsp;
trainer who has collaborated with a plethora&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.660
of celebrity clients. His Instagram features&nbsp;
photos of him training with Brie Larson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.160
Donald Glover, Bradley Cooper,&nbsp;
Pedro Pascal, Miles Teller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.380
and others. Training Blunt for this intense&nbsp;
role was likely just another Tuesday for him.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.240
Walsh's company, Rise Nation, started as a&nbsp;
way for people to train their bodies as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.980
athlete would. Walsh applied that philosophy when&nbsp;
training Blunt. As he put it via Women's Health,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.900
"What I specialize in is figuring out where&nbsp;
the weak points are. It's like building the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.340
foundation of a house. You never, ever&nbsp;
want to have any cracks because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.420
would be carrying around [an 85-pound]&nbsp;
exoskeleton for a lot of the film."

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.800
For Walsh, it was all about building up&nbsp;
Blunt's resilience so the cracks would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.600
never show. He helped strengthen Blunt's&nbsp;
body so she would be prepared to run, jump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.880
and do whatever else she needed to in her suit.&nbsp;
Luckily, she'd already worked with Walsh before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.560
signing on for "Edge of Tomorrow," and Walsh&nbsp;
described their relationship as already having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.760
a decent foundation, leaving them with an&nbsp;
excellent idea of how to work together.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.169
"You're gonna get me there,&nbsp;
and I'm gonna kill it."

00:01:25.169 --> 00:01:31.260
The exoskeleton armor we see Emily Blunt and Tom&nbsp;
Cruise wearing in the film is pretty heavy-duty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.940
It was designed by Pierre Bohanna, who worked&nbsp;
on the "Dark Knight" trilogy creating Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.800
suit. However, he truly outdid himself&nbsp;
while working on "Edge of Tomorrow," as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.320
the massive mechanical suit weighed in at 85&nbsp;
pounds, according to EW. Bohanna shared that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.360
his team made the suit themselves and that it's&nbsp;
made up of over 400 different pieces of machinery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.760
and hardware — over half of them custom-made,&nbsp;
which is what he specializes in. As he told EW,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.260
"You set up a manufacturing process&nbsp;
that's bespoke to it. All the CAD drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.000
all the prototype work, all the molding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.260
the casting, the fabrication, artwork,&nbsp;
and assembly were all done in house."

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:12.060
Bohanna also shared that he looked at real&nbsp;
models to inspire the suits' design — stating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.380
that bionics used to help paraplegics&nbsp;
walk was a big inspiration for his team.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.000
Because of this, Blunt trained to carry an&nbsp;
additional 50 to 60 pounds safely on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.900
body. Jason Walsh shared with Women's Health&nbsp;
that, while he wanted her to be prepared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.060
he also wanted to ensure that she was training&nbsp;
in a safe environment. Above all else, her safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.860
was most important, so she took part in a lot of&nbsp;
stretching and cardio to build up her stamina.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.560
Once Emily Blunt showed up on set for filming,&nbsp;
Tom Cruise was incredibly impressed with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.520
dedication and all of the work she put&nbsp;
in beforehand to bring Rita to life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.960
According to Walsh, Cruise complimented the&nbsp;
actress by saying that he's never worked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.620
someone who came as prepared for the&nbsp;
action scenes as she was. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.400
he wanted them to work out together, in&nbsp;
addition to her training with the stunt crew.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.020
Cruise and Blunt worked out to keep up the&nbsp;
momentum and stay in shape. Blunt shared that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.780
everything about shooting "Edge of Tomorrow" aimed&nbsp;
to capture some realism. Or at least as real as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:11.880
sci-fi film about aliens invading and time travel&nbsp;
can be. Blunt said on the "SmartLess" podcast,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.320
"We had to wear these enormous robotic&nbsp;
suits. I think it would've been great if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.460
they could have CGI'd them, but we wanted&nbsp;
to do it practically in a tactile way."

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.220
In other words, they wanted&nbsp;
it to be as real as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.040
and the only way to do that&nbsp;
was to put in the work.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.960
Cruise has long been known for doing all&nbsp;
his own stunts. Like when he famously held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.540
onto a moving plane in "Mission: Impossible&nbsp;
— Rogue Nation," piloted a real fighter jet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:41.040
for his stunt scenes in "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;
and climbed up the Burj Khalifa in "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.680
Impossible — Ghost Protocol." Some stunt scenes&nbsp;
in Cruise's movies nearly killed him, illustrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.885
his daredevil nature. Now, Blunt can say that&nbsp;
she was right up there with him for this film.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:57.420
In order to increase her endurance, Emily&nbsp;
Blunt worked tirelessly on her cardio. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.360
trainer for "Edge of Tomorrow,"&nbsp;
Jason Walsh, told Women's Health,

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:05.940
"We worked to make sure she had mobility in her&nbsp;
joints, and then flexibility and base strength.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.440
Once her body was resilient and strong enough,&nbsp;
and she had a lot of endurance, I took her out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.320
to the UCLA track to really condition her. She&nbsp;
did tons and tons of sprints up [the] stairs."

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.120
Once Blunt's endurance was good enough, Walsh&nbsp;
began adding pounds to the actress with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.560
weighted vest. However, it was important&nbsp;
to not start off with 60 pounds. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.600
Walsh started with 10 pounds before gradually&nbsp;
moving up the ladder. Once Blunt was ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.680
they moved up to 20 pounds, and&nbsp;
continued to climb at 10-pound intervals.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.480
Walsh argues that training is all about&nbsp;
challenging yourself to find enjoyment — even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.880
when it's hard. And that's the secret sauce that&nbsp;
makes training so powerful, especially for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.100
role like Rita in "Edge of Tomorrow." For Walsh,&nbsp;
it's a good thing when strenuous workouts become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.020
habitual. By then, you're at a place where you can&nbsp;
really push yourself. According to the trainer,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.977
"That's when it becomes fun."

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.580
During her time on the "SmartLess" podcast,&nbsp;
Emily Blunt shared a moment she and Tom Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:08.040
had together while filming "Edge of Tomorrow." She&nbsp;
recalled when she put on the 85-pound exoskeleton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:13.740
armor, she started crying, as it was so heavy and&nbsp;
overwhelming for her. At the time, she didn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.020
to cry in front of everyone, but she felt very&nbsp;
strongly about the cumbersome gear. She expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.400
to Cruise that she wasn't sure how she would be&nbsp;
able to play Rita and was feeling really panicky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.620
about the shoot. She said Cruise was initially&nbsp;
so surprised that he froze, not knowing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.340
to comfort her. However, once he composed&nbsp;
himself, he opted for a joke, telling her,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.620
"Come on, stop being such a pu--y, okay?"

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:39.660
Luckily, that gave her a good laugh and helped&nbsp;
her get into the mindset to do their job. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.200
referred back to the weeks of training she&nbsp;
had just endured to play Rita and was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.400
to step back into the role. While there are&nbsp;
some who have taken offense to this exchange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.480
Blunt has maintained that it was only meant&nbsp;
to motivate her. According to the actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.020
it was a lighthearted moment, not an&nbsp;
offensive one. She shared that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:59.080
joke is something she and Cruise still&nbsp;
laugh about to this day, via E! News.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.980
Due to her extreme training, Emily Blunt&nbsp;
was able to do most of her stunts in "Edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.660
of Tomorrow." However, that did not prepare&nbsp;
her for one of the most intense stunts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:15.000
all — a car crash. During her time on "Conan,"&nbsp;
Blunt detailed a scene she and Tom Cruise share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.540
where she's driving a car at high speed.&nbsp;
She and Cruise are attempting to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:22.440
from a Mimic that's attacking them from&nbsp;
behind. Blunt's character Rita is driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:26.340
while Cruise's character Cage is shooting&nbsp;
at the creature. Blunt shared how she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.880
initially annoyed with Cruise as he was&nbsp;
muttering instructions on how to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:35.100
under his breath, stating that Cruise is not&nbsp;
particularly comfortable being hands-off.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:39.360
"I don't think it's in his fabric to like&nbsp;
being a passenger in a stunt-driving scene."

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.500
However, she quickly learned that he was&nbsp;
trying to help her as he'd done a fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.700
amount of stunt driving before and knew when&nbsp;
it was time for her to hit the brakes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.780
since Blunt was driving the car extremely&nbsp;
fast, per the director's instructions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:55.740
by the time she realized her mistake, it&nbsp;
was too late, and they crashed into a tree.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.580
"As if I thought I knew more about&nbsp;
stunt-driving than Tom Cruise."

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.760
Luckily, the two made it out okay,&nbsp;
with her jokingly telling Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.880
that she found his comments to&nbsp;
be incredibly annoying. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:09.405
they laughed it off and moved on ...&nbsp;
most likely with a different car.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:15.660
"Edge of Tomorrow" was released in 2014, and&nbsp;
since then, Emily Blunt has continued to express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.100
interest in fitness and training. While she was&nbsp;
initially a client of her "Edge of Tomorrow"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.480
trainer Jason Walsh, Blunt has also been working&nbsp;
with trainer Monique Eastwood for her everyday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.980
workouts. According to Women's Health, Blunt&nbsp;
has trained with Eastwood for several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.960
even bringing in her brother-in-law, fellow actor&nbsp;
Stanley Tucci, as one of Eastwood's clients.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:39.120
According to Eastwood's testimonials, she has&nbsp;
racked up quite a few celebrity clients outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.820
Blunt's talented family. Eastwood has worked with&nbsp;
actor Sam Rockwell, downhill skier Jack Gower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:49.740
actress Ruth Wilson, and "Captain America:&nbsp;
The First Avenger" star Hayley Atwell.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.520
Although Blunt trained with Walsh for&nbsp;
"Edge of Tomorrow," Eastwood did tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.180
Women's Health that she helped to prep&nbsp;
Blunt before training with Walsh began.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.536
She shared that they worked on cardio fitness&nbsp;
and muscular endurance to prepare for filming.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.200
In the same year that "Edge&nbsp;
of Tomorrow" was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.820
Emily Blunt also starred in the musical film&nbsp;
"Into The Woods." The actress began filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:13.140
after her work on "Edge of Tomorrow" but had&nbsp;
become pregnant in the interim. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.800
she worked with Monique Eastwood as her&nbsp;
trainer, who took extra care when building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.880
workout plans to compensate for Blunt's&nbsp;
pregnancy with her eldest daughter, Hazel.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.780
Eastwood recalls that she had to adapt her&nbsp;
training regimen to ensure Blunt would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.860
okay with the demands of working on a musical&nbsp;
set. Eastwood shared with Women's Health,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.680
"The priority was to make&nbsp;
sure we did not exhaust her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.160
as she had a singing and dancing&nbsp;
role whilst being pregnant."

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:40.380
Luckily, this was completely in Eastwood's&nbsp;
wheelhouse, as the trainer was once a former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.720
dancer, and working with a pregnant Blunt during&nbsp;
musical numbers was incredibly familiar for her.&nbsp;

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:50.280
It's clear that all the training she&nbsp;
experienced with Walsh for "Edge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.840
Tomorrow" prepared Blunt to tackle the demands of&nbsp;
this role while she was pregnant. And in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.160
the physical requirements for the films&nbsp;
"Into The Woods" and "Edge of Tomorrow"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.460
were impressive hurdles for&nbsp;
the actress to overcome.

